FSINIT(1)                   User Commands                    FSINIT(1)

NAME
    fsinit - mount the filesystems of the boot table

SYNOPSIS
    fsinit [-f fstab] [-v]
    fsinit -m dir

DESCRIPTION
    fsinit reads /etc/fstab and mounts each entry whose mount point is not
    mounted yet. init runs it before the first shell. An entry with the
    seed option is filled from a directory tree when the mounted
    filesystem is empty. The exit status is 1 when a required mount or the
    table itself failed.

    An entry with the homes option contains the home directories. A volume
    of the single user layout, in which the volume itself was the home of
    the one user, is converted first. Its contents move to user/, the
    home of uid 1000, the package prefix .local remains and receives the
    account databases, and the file .layout marks the conversion, which
    happens once. fsinit then makes the missing home of every account in
    /etc/passwd whose home lies on the volume, from /etc/skel.

    After the table fsinit mounts every folder that the host shares
    through virtio-9p. The kernel lists the mount tags in /dev/9p, and
    each share is mounted at /mnt/TAG with the type 9p. A share that
    cannot be mounted is reported and does not change the exit status.

OPTIONS
    -f fstab   read fstab instead of /etc/fstab
    -v         report every entry: mounted, skipped, or mounted already
    -m dir     convert dir from the single user layout and exit

SEE ALSO
    fstab(5), mount(1), useradd(1)
